description:
What: Define Chat struct and compute_chat_id() function in src/chat.rs. Requires sha2 dep (KITH-bhl.1).

Module file: crates/kith-core/src/chat.rs — create this file, then add 'pub mod chat;' to src/lib.rs.

Algorithm:
1. Import: use sha2::{Sha256, Digest};
2. Define Chat struct:
   id: String  (deterministic; computed via compute_chat_id)
   kind: String  ("direct" | "group")
   participants: Vec<String>  (Contact ids)
   created_at: String  → #[serde(rename="createdAt")]  (UTCDate)
   last_message_at: Option<String>  → #[serde(rename="lastMessageAt", skip_serializing_if="Option::is_none")]
   unread_count: u32  → #[serde(rename="unreadCount")]
   Derives: Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize.
3. Implement compute_chat_id:
   pub fn compute_chat_id(participant_tailscale_user_ids: &[impl AsRef<str>]) -> String {
     // a) Collect references, sort lexicographically
     let mut sorted: Vec<&str> = participant_tailscale_user_ids.iter()
       .map(|s| s.as_ref()).collect();
     sorted.sort();
     // b) Join with single null byte separator
     // c) SHA-256 hash of the joined bytes
     let mut hasher = Sha256::new();
     for (i, id) in sorted.iter().enumerate() {
       if i > 0 { hasher.update(b"\x00"); }
       hasher.update(id.as_bytes());
     }
     // d) Hex-encode the 32-byte result (lowercase)
     format!("{:x}", hasher.finalize())
   }
4. Add 'pub mod chat;' to src/lib.rs.
5. Run: cargo check -p kith-core

Defensive programming rules:
- CRITICAL: sorted.sort() must use byte-lexicographic order (Rust's default for &str) — document this
- Empty participant list: function should return the SHA-256 of empty input (deterministic but degenerate)
  Note: callers should reject single-participant chats; this function does not enforce that.
- No unwrap() — Sha256 operations are infallible

Tests (independent oracle: compute expected SHA-256 using openssl or Python beforehand):
  Reference computation (Python):
    import hashlib
    ids = sorted(["uid:alice@example.com", "uid:bob@example.com"])
    data = "\x00".join(ids).encode()
    expected = hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest()

  Test vectors to hardcode (compute before writing tests):
  - Two participants: compute expected hex from Python/openssl
  - Same two participants in reversed order: must produce identical hash
  - Single participant: deterministic hash of one string (no separator)
  - Order independence: ("alice", "bob") == ("bob", "alice")

  The test MUST hardcode the expected hex values computed from Python/openssl — never derive them from the code under test.
